WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.757
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level B two.

2
00:00:06.057 --> 00:00:09.687
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.987 --> 00:00:15.588
Unit four: Modal Verbs of Deduction. Today you will draw logical conclusions from evidence.

4
00:00:15.888 --> 00:00:18.703
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:23.016
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:24.839
Must be.

7
00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:28.616
Must be means strong certainty that it is true.

8
00:00:29.217 --> 00:00:30.508
He must be tired.

9
00:00:31.117 --> 00:00:33.757
Your turn. Say: Must be.

10
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:37.578
Must be.

11
00:00:38.317 --> 00:00:39.341
Might be.

12
00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:42.454
Might be means weak possibility.

13
00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:44.307
She might be late.

14
00:00:44.917 --> 00:00:47.395
Your turn. Say: Might be.

15
00:00:50.233 --> 00:00:51.397
Might be.

16
00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:53.139
Could be.

17
00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:55.964
Could be means possibility.

18
00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:57.760
It could be true.

19
00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:00.561
Your turn. Say: Could be.

20
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:04.308
Could be.

21
00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:05.981
Can't be.

22
00:01:06.983 --> 00:01:09.703
Can't be means strong certainty that it is not true.

23
00:01:10.317 --> 00:01:11.712
That can't be right.

24
00:01:12.317 --> 00:01:15.224
Your turn. Say: Can't be.

25
00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:19.021
Can't be.

26
00:01:19.783 --> 00:01:20.842
Certainty.

27
00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:24.175
Certainty means complete confidence.

28
00:01:24.783 --> 00:01:26.028
I have certainty.

29
00:01:26.617 --> 00:01:29.524
Your turn. Say: Certainty.

30
00:01:32.367 --> 00:01:33.333
Certainty.

31
00:01:34.083 --> 00:01:35.259
Possibility.

32
00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:38.842
Possibility means something that may be true.

33
00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:41.403
A possibility exists.

34
00:01:42.017 --> 00:01:44.681
Your turn. Say: Possibility.

35
00:01:47.533 --> 00:01:48.592
Possibility.

36
00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:50.514
Deduction.

37
00:01:51.517 --> 00:01:54.073
Deduction means a conclusion from evidence.

38
00:01:54.683 --> 00:01:56.207
A logical deduction.

39
00:01:56.817 --> 00:01:59.353
Your turn. Say: Deduction.

40
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:03.143
Deduction.

41
00:02:03.883 --> 00:02:04.815
Evidence.

42
00:02:05.817 --> 00:02:08.699
Evidence means facts that support a conclusion.

43
00:02:09.283 --> 00:02:10.505
There is evidence.

44
00:02:11.117 --> 00:02:13.688
Your turn. Say: Evidence.

45
00:02:16.533 --> 00:02:17.453
Evidence.

46
00:02:18.267 --> 00:02:21.929
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:22.783 --> 00:02:27.897
Visal, look at this clue: the lights are on but nobody answers the door. What do you deduce?

48
00:02:28.983 --> 00:02:35.270
They must be at home, but they might be sleeping or they could be busy. Must is for strong evidence.

49
00:02:36.383 --> 00:02:37.965
And for negative deduction?

50
00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:47.774
Can't be means I am almost certain it is not true: He can't be the thief; he was abroad. Never use mustn't for deduction.

51
00:02:48.883 --> 00:02:50.326
How does the scale work?

52
00:02:51.417 --> 00:03:01.035
The more evidence you have, the stronger the modal. In writing, we support the deduction with the evidence itself: The lights are on, so someone must be home.

53
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.828
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:04.683 --> 00:03:09.887
Grammar focus: Deduction: must and might and could and can't plus be.

55
00:03:10.187 --> 00:03:11.421
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:12.117 --> 00:03:14.197
The lights are on, so they must be at home.

57
00:03:15.083 --> 00:03:17.489
He was abroad, so he can't be the thief.

58
00:03:18.383 --> 00:03:20.487
I'm not sure, but she might be sleeping.

59
00:03:21.433 --> 00:03:32.032
Must = almost certain. Can't = almost certain it's not true. Might / could = possible. Use evidence to choose the modal; mustn't is not used for deduction.

60
00:03:32.783 --> 00:03:35.900
Be careful! Don't say: He mustn't be at home.

61
00:03:37.867 --> 00:03:39.843
Say: He can't be at home.

62
00:03:40.143 --> 00:03:41.864
Use can't for deduction.

63
00:03:42.667 --> 00:03:45.412
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:47.515
Following the Clues.

65
00:03:47.815 --> 00:03:50.128
The lights are on, so someone must be at home.

66
00:03:51.133 --> 00:03:54.224
The door is closed, so they might be sleeping.

67
00:03:55.233 --> 00:03:58.022
He was abroad last week, so he can't be the thief.

68
00:03:59.033 --> 00:04:02.345
It could be a misunderstanding, but we need more evidence.

69
00:04:03.333 --> 00:04:06.297
A good deduction always depends on clear evidence.

70
00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:11.332
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:12.183 --> 00:04:14.778
The lights are on. They blank be at home.

72
00:04:15.078 --> 00:04:18.371
A, must. B, mustn't. C, can.

73
00:04:21.933 --> 00:04:23.782
The answer is A: must.

74
00:04:24.917 --> 00:04:27.450
He was in Japan, so he blank have been here.

75
00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:30.475
A, must. B, can't. C, should.

76
00:04:34.033 --> 00:04:36.079
The answer is B: can't.

77
00:04:37.217 --> 00:04:39.985
I'm not sure. She blank be tired.

78
00:04:40.285 --> 00:04:43.451
A, might. B, must. C, will.

79
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:48.628
The answer is A: might.

80
00:04:49.783 --> 00:04:51.644
Which is the strongest certainty?

81
00:04:51.944 --> 00:04:55.969
A, might be. B, could be. C, must be.

82
00:04:59.533 --> 00:05:01.544
The answer is C: must be.

83
00:05:02.683 --> 00:05:05.705
According to the reading, what does a good deduction depend on?

84
00:05:06.005 --> 00:05:09.089
A, evidence. B, luck. C, opinion.

85
00:05:12.633 --> 00:05:14.749
The answer is A: evidence.

86
00:05:15.933 --> 00:05:18.876
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:19.717 --> 00:05:23.098
Look around you and make a deduction using 'must be' or 'might be'.

88
00:05:31.883 --> 00:05:33.360
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:42.771
The cafe is full and people are queuing, so the food must be good. The owner might be a famous chef. I use must and might to show different levels of certainty.

90
00:05:43.071 --> 00:05:44.687
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:05:45.583 --> 00:05:52.055
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and deduction: must and might and could and can't plus be.

92
00:05:52.355 --> 00:05:57.341
Today's value is logical rigor. Follow your reasoning wherever it honestly leads.

93
00:05:57.641 --> 00:06:00.305
See you in Unit five. Goodbye!

94
00:06:00.605 --> 00:06:03.815
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

